.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt LOOPCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm loopctl
.Nd attach, detach, or list loop devices
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Cm attach
.Op Fl r
.Op Ar N
.Ar file
.Nm
.Cm detach
.Ar N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command attaches a regular file to a loop device, detaches a device,
or lists attachments.
The four devices are
.Pa /dev/loop0
through
.Pa /dev/loop3 .
A file system in an attached file can be mounted using the loop device
as the source for
.Xr mount 8 .
.Pp
With no arguments,
.Nm
prints
.Pa /proc/loop .
Each attached device has one line giving its name, size in bytes,
whether it accepts writes, write count, sync count, and the path used
to attach the file.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm attach Oo Fl r Oc Oo Ar N Oc Ar file
Attach the file to device
.Ar N ,
or to the first free device when
.Ar N
is omitted.
A relative
.Ar file
is taken from the current directory, as
.Xr realpath 3
resolves it, since the kernel takes an absolute path.
The
.Fl r
option makes the device read-only.
Every mount of that device is read-only, regardless of the requested options.
.It Cm detach Ar N
Detach device
.Ar N .
.El
.Pp
Only root can attach files.
The program writes one command at a time to
.Pa /dev/loop-control :
.Ql attach N /path ,
.Ql attach - /path ,
.Ql attach ro N /path ,
or
.Ql detach N .
The control node is owned by root with mode 0600.
The loop nodes are block devices.
A node also reads and writes its device in whole sectors, as
.Xr loop 4
describes.
.Pp
Attachment resolves and opens the file under its volume's namespace lock,
syncs it, and rechecks its length and busy state before publishing the device.
If a writer relocates the file while attachment waits for the lock,
attachment resolves the relocated file.
If the path now resolves on another volume, attachment fails with
.Er EBUSY .
.Pp
An attached file cannot be written or truncated; these operations fail with
.Er EBUSY .
The volume containing the file cannot be unmounted until the device is
detached.
Removing a USB stick also removes loop devices attached to files on it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/loop-control
.It Pa /dev/loop-control
Control node.
.It Pa /dev/loop0 No through Pa /dev/loop3
Loop devices.
.It Pa /proc/loop
Attached devices and their counters.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is 0 on success, 1 on an error, and 2 on invalid syntax.
.Sh EXAMPLES
With device 0 free,
.Dl loopctl attach /demo/ext2.img
prints nothing.
.Dl loopctl
lists loop0.
Mount the file system, then unmount it before detaching:
.Dl mount -t ext2 /dev/loop0 /mnt
.Dl umount /mnt
.Dl loopctl detach 0
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Invalid syntax prints the following on standard error:
.Dl usage: loopctl [attach [-r] [N] FILE | detach N]
Kernel errors are reported as
.Ql loopctl: /dev/loop-control: ERROR
when the control node cannot be opened, or
.Ql loopctl: ERROR
when a command fails.
Failure to open
.Pa /proc/loop
is reported as
.Ql loopctl: /proc/loop: ERROR .
.Pp
Errors include:
.Bl -tag -width EACCES
.It Er EACCES
The caller is not root.
.It Er EBUSY
The file is already attached, even through another hard link;
shared mappings of it hold its pages in memory, which a write through the
device would not reach;
a description remains busy when attachment checks it;
the path resolves on another volume after the lock wait;
the device has a mount or a held buffer;
or a detach was attempted while an attachment was in progress.
.It Er ELOOP
The chain of devices under the file is too deep: the kernel counts the
links to the backing device, so one loop device may stand on a disk,
while an image on the RAM root allows two nested loop devices.
.It Er EINVAL
The file length is not a whole number of sectors,
the device number is outside 0 through 3,
or the file is not regular.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr loop 4 ,
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr mount 8
